KT=('Kerala Tourism: Various types of Theyyam','https://keralatourism.org/bekal/theyyam-various-types.php')
CATS=[
 ('gods','Gods and guardians','Deities of the Shaiva and Vaishnava traditions, village guardians and protective spirits.'),
 ('goddesses','Goddesses','The Bhagavathis and other mother goddesses, from gentle and graceful to fierce.'),
 ('heroes','Heroes and ancestors','Ordinary people, warriors and wronged women who are worshipped as divine after their death.'),
 ('harmony','Shared traditions','Forms that tell of Hindu and Muslim traditions meeting in North Malabar.'),
]
T=[
dict(slug='muthappan',name='Muthappan',ml='മുത്തപ്പൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='The approachable grandfather god of Kannur, worshipped by people of every background.',
 alias=['Thiruvappana','Vellatom','Valiya Muthappan','Cheriya Muthappan','Madappura'],tags=['kannur','toddy','dogs','compassion','people','shiva'],
 facts=[('Category','God'),('Also known as','Thiruvappana and Vellatom'),('Associated with','Kannur district and the Madappura shrines'),('People pray for','Protection, health, livelihood, comfort')],
 who='Muthappan means “grandfather” in Malayalam, and that is how he is approached: as a close, forgiving elder who is easy to talk to. He is worshipped as two forms of one deity, Thiruvappana and Vellatom, and is often linked with Shiva. His rites are traditionally led by the Thiyya community, who regard him as their group ancestor.',
 story=['A well-known story says that a childless nobleman and his wife, devotees of Shiva, found a beautiful baby boy on a rock and raised him as their son. As he grew he took food to poor families and mixed freely with everyone, which went against the customs of the household. When he revealed that he was divine, he left home. Because his gaze burned whatever it touched, he is said to have pierced his own eyes to spare the world, which is why one form is shown as blind.','On his journey he was drawn to the toddy palms of a village. A toddy tapper who tried to catch him stealing found himself unable to move, and his wife recognised the old man in the palm tree and called him “Muthappan”. She offered him boiled gram, coconut slices, grilled fish and toddy, and he made the place his home.'],
 look='Muthappan appears with a painted face and a tall headdress, and a calm, kindly manner. The Theyyam sits and talks with devotees, listens to their troubles and gives blessings. Dogs are closely associated with him.',
 trad='The shrines, called madappuras, have no idol and no flagstaff. Offerings are distinctive, including boiled gram, coconut and, in some practice, toddy and fish. Entry is not limited by caste or religion, and people of many communities take part. Unlike most Theyyam, Muthappan is performed through the year, not only in the main season.',
 note='Muthappan is a particularly personal deity. Visitors should expect devotees to approach him with quiet affection and should take their cue from the people around them.',
 src=[('Muthappan','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muthappan')]),
dict(slug='vishnumoorthi',name='Vishnumoorthi',ml='വിഷ്ണുമൂർത്തി',cat='gods',
 tag='A powerful form of Vishnu, remembered through the story of the devotee Palanthai Kannan.',
 alias=['Vishnumurthy','Narasimhamoorthi','Palanthai Kannan','Vishnumoorthy'],tags=['vishnu','narasimha','vaishnava','neeleswaram','kannan','headdress','kolam'],
 facts=[('Category','God (Vaishnava)'),('Also known as','Narasimhamoorthi'),('Associated with','Nileshwar and the Tulu coast'),('Remembered for','The story of Palanthai Kannan and the Narasimha avatar')],
 who='Vishnumoorthi is one of the two main Vaishnava deities in Theyyam. Scholars note that Vaishnavism arrived late in Kerala’s folk worship, so Vishnumoorthi stands out in a tradition mostly shaped by Shaiva and Shakta forms. The deity is also called Narasimhamoorthi because the performance retells Vishnu’s avatar as Narasimha, the half-man, half-lion.',
 story=['The ritual remembers Palanthai Kannan, a devoted follower of Vishnu. As a boy he was beaten and driven out of his village for picking mangoes from a landlord’s tree. He found refuge in a Vishnu shrine on the coast, and after many years he was told to return home. On his return he was killed. Vishnu, who had accompanied his devotee, destroyed the landlord’s house in anger.','An astrologer then told the landlord that the god was displeased and ordered a shrine and a ritual to be made for him. From then on the god was worshipped as Vishnumoorthi. A second reading of the Theyyam sees it as Narasimha emerging from a pillar to protect the child Prahlada from his father, the demon king Hiranyakashipu.'],
 look='The deity wears a tall, red headdress, a heavy red costume and a long skirt of palm-leaf strips. The drumming is loud and unmistakable, and the movements are strong and sudden. Many viewers say the Narasimha part of the performance is among the most dramatic in North Malabar.',
 trad='The Theyyam is held at many shrines and family homes. In some forms the performer takes part in a ritual with fire, which is part of the deity’s tradition, and the performance is traditionally by members of the Malaya community.',
 note='Because the movements are fast and the crowd presses in, a guide will place you where you can see safely. Stand back when asked.',
 src=[('Vishnumoorthy Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vishnumoorthy_Theyyam'),('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='pottan',name='Pottan',ml='പൊട്ടൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='The god who answered a scholar’s pride with a question about equality.',
 alias=['Pottan Theyyam','Shiva as Pottan'],tags=['shiva','shankaracharya','equality','caste','message','philosophy'],
 facts=[('Category','God'),('Often linked with','Shiva'),('Remembered for','The meeting with Adi Shankara'),('Message','Everyone is equal')],
 who='Pottan is a form of the divine that speaks against caste discrimination. In many tellings he is understood as Shiva in the guise of a low-caste man. Pottan is a lively, colourful performance and an important part of the cultural heritage of the old Kolathunadu region.',
 story=['The legend says that Pottan was walking along the road with a child on his hip and a pot of toddy on his head when he met the scholar Adi Shankara. The scholar told him to leave the road so that he would not be polluted. Pottan asked why. He pointed out that the scholar crossed the same rivers, offered the same bananas to his god and wore the same garlands, and that both men bleed the same blood when wounded.','In the story Shankara realises his error and asks for forgiveness. The Theyyam keeps the question alive as a lesson in humility and equality.'],
 look='Pottan wears a skirt of palm-leaf strips, a small headdress and a painted face, and carries a pot. His performance is lively and vivid. After the ritual, devotees may speak to him and receive his answer.',
 trad='Some families keep a small shrine called a palliyara for Pottan. His performances often include a large fire for the deity to rest beside, and people may call on him to get up.',
 note='Pottan is both funny and serious. Laugh when others laugh, and keep your voice low during the ritual song.',
 src=[('Pottan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pottan_Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='gulikan',name='Gulikan',ml='ഗുളികൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='A fierce guardian linked to time, fate and Yama.',
 alias=['Guliga','Karingulikan','Karim Gulikan','Gulikan Theyyam','Guliga Daiva','Gulika'],tags=['yama','shani','guardian','fate','stilts','death','tulu'],
 facts=[('Category','Guardian deity'),('Also known as','Guliga (Tulu Nadu)'),('Linked with','Yama, god of death, and Shani'),('Shrines','Small shrines called Gulikan kavus')],
 who='Gulikan is a powerful guardian worshipped in Kerala and, as Guliga, in the Tulu-speaking region of coastal Karnataka. He is linked in astrology with Shani and is understood as an aspect of Yama, the god of death. His shrines are usually small groves called kavus.',
 story=['Gulikan belongs to the old tradition of guardian deities who watch over a place and its people. Because of his link with time, fate and death, he is approached with respect and some caution, and is asked for protection rather than favours.'],
 look='Gulikan is among the most striking performances: the face is painted in sharp, stylised patterns, the movement is fast and forceful, and in some performances the Theyyam rises on tall stilts above a long palm-leaf skirt.',
 trad='Gulikan is part of the wider kaliyattam tradition and has parallels in the Bhuta Kola worship of Tulu Nadu.',
 note='Keep a respectful distance during Gulikan’s appearance, as the movements are wide and fast.',
 src=[('Gulikan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulikan_Theyyam'),('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='kuttichathan',name='Kuttichathan',ml='കുട്ടിച്ചാത്തൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='A fiery child guardian said to be the son of Shiva and Parvati.',
 alias=['Kalakattu Kuttichathan','Shasthappan','Sasthappan','Chathan','Kuttichathan Theyyam'],tags=['child','guardian','shiva','parvati','folklore','payyannur','red','mischief'],
 facts=[('Category','Guardian deity'),('Also known as','Shasthappan, Kalakattu Kuttichathan'),('Believed to be','A son of Shiva and Parvati'),('Appearance','Red costume, peacock-like back ornament')],
 who='Kuttichathan is a widely loved guardian figure in Malabar folklore. He is often described as a mischievous but powerful child of the gods. The Theyyam is also called Shasthappan and, because it is associated with the Kalakattu family, Kalakattu Kuttichathan.',
 story=['One popular story says that Shiva and Parvati, disguised as a hunter couple, had two children and left the younger, Kuttichathan, with a childless Namboodiri family. The clever child questioned his teacher until the teacher had no answer, and the family later turned against him. He was killed, but returned in many forms, and the people who had wronged him decided to worship him as a Theyyam.','Another story tells of a child born to a Namboodiri and a woman of a lower community, who was raised in secret and gave grain to the poor. In every telling he is a figure who stands for people who were excluded or treated unfairly.'],
 look='Kuttichathan wears a red costume, a headdress with a piece of wood on each cheek, and a metal cap over the eyes with a small hole to see through. A fan of feathers rises from the back like a peacock’s tail.',
 trad='Devotees believe that Kuttichathan can grant wishes when asked with faith.',
 note='The costume is heavy and the performer’s vision is limited, so keep clear of the dancing area.',
 src=[('Kuttichathan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuttichathan_Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='poomaruthan',name='Poomaruthan',ml='പൂമരുതൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='The brotherly guardian who travelled with the goddess Poomala.',
 alias=['Poomaruthan Theyyam','Poomala Bhagavathi','Poomala'],tags=['wind','flower','sword','shield','brother','thiyya','ship'],
 facts=[('Category','God'),('Companion of','The goddess Poomala'),('Carries','A sword and a wooden shield'),('Worshipped mostly by','The Thiyya community')],
 who='Poomaruthan is a god of North Malabar who is worshipped together with the goddess Poomala. His name joins “poo”, a flower, and “maruthan”, wind.',
 story=['Poomala was admiring a heavenly garden and wanted to pick flowers, but the guards stopped her. She asked for help from one of the guards, who was resting in a blossom in the form of wind. He helped, and became her brotherly friend. They later travelled together to Malanadu in a great wooden ship built by Vishwakarma and came ashore on the North Malabar coast, where they settled and are worshipped.'],
 look='Poomaruthan has a painted face with a black moustache and beard, and a very large wooden headdress. He carries a sword and a wooden shield and dances while spinning the shield. All the materials for the costume and face paint come from natural sources.',
 trad='Poomaruthan is performed in the shrines of Poomala Bhagavathi, who herself does not appear as a Theyyam. In some practice, assistants gather and shout to provoke the Theyyam, who may tap them with his shield.',
 note='The shield and sword are part of the ritual. Do not stand within the assistants’ circle.',
 src=[('Poomaruthan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poomaruthan_Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='muchilot-bhagavathi',name='Muchilot Bhagavathi',ml='മുച്ചിലോട്ട് ഭഗവതി',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A learned woman who became a goddess, and one of the most graceful of all Theyyams.',
 alias=['Muchilottu Bhagavathi','Muchilottu Bhagavathy','Muchilot Bhagavathy'],tags=['vaniya','gentle','graceful','scholar','food','kaliyattam','108'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Family deity of','The Vaniya community'),('Nature','Gentle and graceful'),('Known for','Annadanam, the serving of food')],
 who='Muchilot Bhagavathi is the family deity of the Vaniya community in North Malabar. Her shrines are traditionally numbered at 108, echoing the 108 Shiva temples of the Shivalaya Stotram. She is widely described as the most elaborate and beautiful of all Theyyams.',
 story=['The best-known story tells of a gifted young Brahmin woman of Perinchellur who defeated senior scholars in debate. Jealous, they challenged her character and drove her out. She took refuge at a temple and asked to be consumed by fire. A Vaniya man carrying oil passed by, and she asked him to pour it over her. With Shiva’s blessing she returned as a goddess.','Later she revealed herself to a soldier of the Kolathiri king and his wife, who began to worship her as their family deity. A sacred manuscript of her tradition tells it differently, as a goddess created by Shiva for the good of the world.'],
 look='Muchilot Bhagavathi wears a very large, round headdress with intricate red and silver detail and a richly layered costume. Her movements are slow and gentle, reflecting her calm, graceful nature.',
 trad='Serving food to everyone present is an important part of the festivals of Muchilot Bhagavathi.',
 note='If you are offered food, accept it with thanks. Guests are part of the occasion.',
 src=[('Muchilot Bhagavathi','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muchilot_Bhagavathi')]),
dict(slug='uchitta',name='Uchitta Bhagavathy',ml='ഉച്ചിട്ട',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A beloved goddess whom women pray to for safe childbirth.',
 alias=['Uchitta','Uchitta Bhagavathi','Adiyeri Madathil Uchitta','Vadakkinakathachi','Uchitta Amma'],tags=['women','childbirth','agni','parvati','krishna','kamsa','mother'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Also known as','Adiyeri Madathil Uchitta Bhagavathy'),('People pray for','Safe delivery, family wellbeing'),('Performed by','The Malaya and Velar communities')],
 who='Uchitta is a regional goddess of Kannur and Kasaragod who is much loved by women, who pray to her for a safe delivery. Her name is said to come from her loud voice, “uchathil” in Malayalam.',
 story=['There are several stories. In one she is the goddess whom the tyrant Kamsa tried to kill in place of Krishna, who then called out that his killer had already been born. In another she is the daughter of Agni, the god of fire, born from an ember that fell on Brahma’s lotus, and she came to earth at the request of the goddess Bhumi. Some say she is another form of Parvati, others say she is a daughter of Shiva.'],
 look='Uchitta appears in a red costume with an orange-red painted face and a skirt of palm-leaf strips, with a decorated crown. The performance is playful and personal, and the goddess takes time to speak with devotees.',
 trad='Because she is said to be Agni’s daughter, her ritual includes moments of play with embers. She also appears as part of larger Theyyam festivals in many temples.',
 note='Uchitta is approached with warmth. Expect families, especially mothers and expecting mothers, to come forward for her blessing.',
 src=[('Uchitta Bhagavathy','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uchitta_Bhagavathy')]),
dict(slug='vasoorimala',name='Vasoorimala',ml='വസൂരിമാല',cat='goddesses',
 tag='The goddess associated with epidemic disease, worshipped for healing.',
 alias=['Vasoorimala Theyyam','Vasurimala','Manodari'],tags=['smallpox','disease','healing','bhadrakali','darikan','epidemic','health'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Associated with','Smallpox and contagious illness'),('Also called','Companion of Bhadrakali'),('People pray for','Healing and protection from disease')],
 who='Vasoorimala is a female deity of Kerala and the Kodagu region who is worshipped as a sub-deity in Bhadrakali and Shiva temples. “Vasoori” is the Malayalam word for smallpox, and her name means “garland of pox”. In the old understanding, people honoured the divine forces that brought disease so that they might also take it away.',
 story=['Folklore from the Aithihyamala tells that Manodari, wife of the demon Darikan, did penance to Shiva when her husband was losing to Bhadrakali. Shiva gave her his sweat with a blessing. In her anger she sprayed it on Bhadrakali, who broke out in pox. Bhadrakali named her Vasoorimala and made her a companion. Another account says she arose from Shiva’s own consciousness when he suffered smallpox.'],
 look='Vasoorimala appears in a red costume and a painted face with prominent decoration and a garland around the neck. She is performed in the temples of North Kerala, and her ritual is a prayer for the health of the community.',
 trad='When smallpox spread widely, people began to worship the disease as a Theyyam. Today the Theyyam is performed for the cure of illness.',
 note='Many devotees come to Vasoorimala with personal prayers. Keep a respectful distance from those seeking her blessing.',
 src=[('Vasoorimala','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vasoorimala')]),
dict(slug='arya-poonkani',name='Arya Poonkani',ml='ആര്യപൂങ്കന്നി',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A goddess who crossed the sea and found a friend in a Muslim sailor.',
 alias=['Aryakkarakani','Aryapoonkanni','Arya Poonkanni','Aryapoomkunniyamma'],tags=['sea','ship','pearls','bappiriyan','harmony','muslim','wooden footwear'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Companion','Bappiriyan, the sailor'),('Notable detail','Wears wooden footwear'),('Story','A voyage, a shipwreck and an unlikely friendship')],
 who='Arya Poonkani is a goddess of North Malabar whose story links two faiths. She is performed together with Bappiriyan, a sailor who is believed to have been a Muslim.',
 story=['Arya Poonkani was born to Aryapattar and Aryapattathi and grew fond of ornaments and pearls. She and her brothers set out by ship to find pearls for her marriage, but were caught in a storm and separated by a shipwreck. After seven days at sea they came ashore. Alone, she saw a sailor, Bappiriyan, in a small boat. He ignored her at first, but she showed her powers and he agreed to help her look for her brothers.','They found the brothers, who chose to settle where they were. Arya Poonkani and Bappiriyan went on to the Kooran hills on the North Malabar coast and settled there. Many shrines were later built across the region.'],
 look='Her face art is delicate and her movements are slow. A large, round, tiered red headdress rises behind her. Unlike most Theyyam, who perform barefoot, she wears traditional wooden footwear.',
 trad='Bappiriyan is usually performed in the same shrine, and the pair are often cited as an example of Hindu-Muslim harmony in Kerala.',
 note='Look for the wooden footwear. It is one of the details that sets this Theyyam apart.',
 src=[('Arya Poonkani','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arya_Poonkani'),('Bappiriyan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bappiriyan_Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='chamundi',name='Chamundi',ml='ചാമുണ്ഡി',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A fierce warrior goddess, honoured in several forms across the region.',
 alias=['Madayil Chamundi','Moovalamkuzhi Chamundi','Patten Chamundi','Raktha Chamundi','Kundora Chamundi','Rakta Chamundi','Rakteshwari','Kundadi Chamundi','Chamundi Theyyam'],tags=['kali','warrior','darika','durga','fierce','velan','halo','family deity'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Forms','Madayil, Rakta and Kundora Chamundi, among others'),('Linked with','Kali and the slaying of demons'),('Role','Village and household guardian')],
 who='Chamundi is the fierce, protective form of the goddess, worshipped in several local forms. Three well-known types are Madayil Chamundi, Rakta Chamundi and Kundora Chamundi. Chamundi shrines are often kept by ruling families and households alongside those of Kurathi and Vishnumoorthi.',
 story=['In the old stories, Chamundi is identified with Kali, who killed the demons Chanda and Munda and drank the blood of Raktabija so that none reached the ground. In one form, she followed her enemies into the underworld, which is why she is called Madayil Chamundi.','Kundora Chamundi is understood as a fierce form of Kali who defeated the demon Darika. She is honoured as a village guardian and a household deity, and is traditionally performed by the Velan community.'],
 look='Many forms of Chamundi have a very large, round, halo-like headdress edged with palm leaf and decorated in red, with a fierce painted face and rich ornaments.',
 trad='As a warrior goddess she is a figure of both protection and fear, and rituals are shaped by older traditions of sacrifice and offering.',
 note='Chamundi’s large round headdress is heavy and wide. Keep well clear when the Theyyam turns.',
 src=[('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam'),('Chamunda','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamunda')]),
dict(slug='kathivanoor-veeran',name='Kathivanoor Veeran',ml='കതിവനൂർ വീരൻ',cat='heroes',
 tag='The warrior Mandappan Chekavar, remembered as a hero and honoured as a god.',
 alias=['Mandappan Chekavar','Mannappan','Kathivannoor Veeran','Kathivannur Veeran','Kathivanur Veeran','Mandhappan Chekavar'],tags=['warrior','thiyya','hero','chemmarathi','banana','kodagu','marriage','husband','fire'],
 facts=[('Category','Hero deity'),('Real name','Mandappan Chekavar'),('Remembered for','Courage in defence of his village'),('Women pray for','A healthy husband')],
 who='Kathivanoor Veeran is one of the most popular Theyyams of North Malabar. “Veeran” means hero. He is remembered as Mandappan Chekavar, a Thiyya warrior whose life and death are still told in the folklore of the Kolathunadu region.',
 story=['Mandappan was born with the blessing of the goddess Chuzali and grew up skilled in martial arts. He preferred hunting in the woods to working, and his father, angry, broke his bow. Mandappan left home, went to live with his uncle, did well in business and married Chemmarathi.','When news came that an army from Kodagu was about to attack, he took up arms and won the battle. On the way home he realised he had lost a ring and a little finger and went back for them. Enemy soldiers killed him by treachery. Chemmarathi, on seeing the ring and finger, joined his funeral pyre. Afterwards, the two were seen as divine, and his uncle gave the Theyyam its name, Kathivanoor Veeran.'],
 look='Kathivanoor Veeran is known for dynamic, flexible movement. The face carries a beard and moustache in a style called nakam thazhthi ezhuthu. The Theyyam is traditionally performed at night or very early morning. A raised structure made of banana stems, called Chemmarathi thara, represents his wife. Its sixty-four cells recall that his body was said to have been cut into sixty-four pieces.',
 trad='Many young women of North Malabar pray to Kathivanoor Veeran for a healthy husband. A performer is expected to know a great deal, as visitors may put questions to the deity and are given answers.',
 note='Kathivanoor Veeran’s ritual involves a lot of fire and movement. Follow your guide’s advice on where to stand.',
 src=[('Kathivanoor Veeran','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kathivanoor_Veeran')]),
dict(slug='pulimaranja-thondachan',name='Pulimaranja Thondachan',ml='പുലിമറഞ്ഞ തൊണ്ടച്ചൻ',cat='heroes',
 tag='A gifted man who learned to become a leopard, and was wronged for it.',
 alias=['Kari Kurikal','Madai Kari Kurikkal','Pulimaranja Thondachan Theyyam','Tiger Who Disappeared'],tags=['leopard','tiger','magic','pulayar','kalari','pulijanmam','ancestor'],
 facts=[('Category','Hero and ancestor'),('Also known as','Kari Kurikal'),('Worshipped by','Mainly the Pulayar community'),('Name means','The ancestor who vanished as a tiger')],
 who='Pulimaranja Thondachan is worshipped mainly by the Pulayar community of Kannur and Kasaragod. “Thondachan” is a word for grandfather in North Malabar. The name means the ancestor who vanished as a tiger.',
 story=['The legend says that Kari, a man of the Pulaya community, was refused entry to the martial-arts school because of his caste. A landlord helped by letting him use his name, and Kari went on to train in many kalaris, mastering the art of changing form. He became a respected kurikkal, a teacher and healer.','A distant landlord asked him to cure his madness and promised half his property, then plotted to kill him instead of paying. Kari had to bring a tiger, its milk and its hair as a condition. He told his wife not to be afraid when he returned as a leopard, but she was frightened and closed the door. He then disappeared into the forest. The landlord’s illness returned, and people came to worship Kari as Pulimaranja Thondachan.'],
 look='The Theyyam wears a large, wide, tiered headdress and a layered red costume, with a painted face and ornaments. Its dance recalls the leopard.',
 trad='The story has inspired a well-known play and film, both called Pulijanmam.',
 note='This is a story about injustice and dignity. Listen quietly while the song is sung.',
 src=[('Pulimaranja Thondachan','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulimaranja_Thondachan')]),
dict(slug='kadangot-makkam',name='Kadangot Makkam',ml='കടങ്കോട്ട് മാക്കം',cat='heroes',
 tag='A wronged sister and her children, who became deities after their death.',
 alias=['Makkam','Makkappothi','Makkavum Makkalum','Kadangott Makkam'],tags=['mother','children','brothers','innocence','woman','justice','kunhimangalam'],
 facts=[('Category','Heroic woman and deity'),('Also known as','Makkappothi, Makkavum Makkalum'),('Worshipped with','Her two children and a companion'),('People pray for','Children, justice and family wellbeing')],
 who='Kadangot Makkam is a woman of the North Malabar region who is worshipped as a goddess after being wronged. Her Theyyam is also called Makkappothi, or “Makkam and her children”.',
 story=['Makkam was the only sister of twelve warrior brothers and was loved by all. While the brothers were away at war, their wives, who disliked her, spread a false story about her. The brothers believed it and decided to kill her. On a journey with her two children, Makkam stopped to drink at a well. There her brothers killed her and the children and threw them into the well. A passer-by who had witnessed it was killed as well.','Later, she took the form of a fierce deity, and the family home was destroyed by fire. The local people became convinced of her innocence and began to worship her, her children and the man who died with them.'],
 look='Makkam is performed together with her children and the man who died with her, each in their own costume.',
 trad='Families who wish for children sometimes sponsor a Makkam Theyyam. She is also performed as part of the annual festivals of her shrines.',
 note='This is a story of grief as well as justice. A quiet and respectful manner is especially important.',
 src=[('Kadangot Makkam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadangot_Makkam')]),
dict(slug='bappiriyan',name='Bappiriyan',ml='ബാപ്പിരിയൻ',cat='harmony',
 tag='A sailor who is believed to have been a Muslim, honoured in a Hindu ritual.',
 alias=['Bappirian','Bappiriyan Theyyam','Mappila Theyyam','Hanuman Theyyam'],tags=['muslim','mappila','sailor','harmony','coconut tree','ship','arya poonkani','hanuman'],
 facts=[('Category','Mappila Theyyam'),('Companion of','Arya Poonkani'),('Performed by','The Vannan community'),('Signature act','Climbing and shaking a coconut tree')],
 who='Bappiriyan is a Theyyam of the Mappila tradition. He is believed to be a Muslim merchant and sea captain and is remembered alongside the goddess Arya Poonkani. The pairing is often cited as an example of Hindu-Muslim harmony in Kerala.',
 story=['In one telling, Bappiriyan was the captain of the ship in which the goddess Arya Poonkani sailed. After a shipwreck she called on him for help, and they travelled together to the Kooran hills, where both are enshrined.','Another telling links him with the Ramayana, as Hanuman searching for Sita. Unable to find her, he climbs tall trees to look farther, and in anger kicks the coconut tree so that its nuts fall.'],
 look='Bappiriyan wears a striking, tall red headdress, a painted face and a long skirt of palm leaf, and his ritual includes climbing a coconut tree.',
 trad='The climb is difficult and the performer can be injured, which is why guides keep visitors well back from the tree.',
 note='If the performer climbs, stay out of the area under the tree. Falling coconuts are part of the story, but they can hurt.',
 src=[('Bappiriyan Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bappiriyan_Theyyam'),('Arya Poonkani','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arya_Poonkani')]),

dict(slug='theechamundi',name='Theechamundi',ml='തീച്ചാമുണ്ഡി',cat='gods',
 tag='A Theyyam of devotion and fire, remembered through the story of Narasimha and Prahlada.',
 alias=['Thee Chamundi','Thee Chamundi Ottakolam','Ottakolam','Theechamundi Theyyam'],tags=['fire','narasimha','prahlada','vishnu','agni','ottakolam','devotion','embers'],
 facts=[('Category','God (Vaishnava tradition)'),('Also known as','Thee Chamundi, Ottakolam'),('Linked with','Narasimha, Prahlada and Agni, the fire god'),('Performed by','Traditionally the Malaya community')],
 who='Theechamundi is a fire-linked Theyyam that is connected in most accounts with Vishnu in his Narasimha form. Although the name carries “Chamundi”, which is usually a goddess, the tellings we have found centre on Vishnu and fire. Accounts differ from shrine to shrine, so please treat this as a general summary. It is also known as Ottakolam, a name used for a Theyyam that appears alone.',
 story=['One telling links it to the story of the child devotee Prahlada, whom his father, the demon king Hiranyakashipu, tried to destroy by fire, among other trials. Vishnu protected the boy and appeared as Narasimha, the half-man, half-lion.','Another telling says that after Narasimha’s victory the worlds rejoiced, but Agni, the god of fire, did not join in and made light of the deed. Vishnu, angered, entered the sacrificial fire and subdued Agni. A third account says Shiva created a fire from his third eye, which Narasimha entered to cool his rage. All the versions are oral tradition and have no single written source.'],
 look='The performer wears a costume of palm-leaf strips and a painted face, with a decorated headdress. The ritual centres on fire, and it is one of the most intense of all Theyyam performances.',
 trad='The deity is approached with devotion and fear, and it is understood as an expression of the god’s protective power rather than a display. As with other Theyyams, devotees may receive a blessing afterwards.',
 note='This is a high-risk ritual. Always keep to the boundary set by the organisers and your guide, and never move closer to the fire for a better view.',
 src=[('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam'),('Hindu Blog: Thee Chamundi Theyyam','https://www.hindu-blog.com/2023/06/thee-chamundi-theyyam-story-information.html')]),
dict(slug='karimchamundi',name='Karimchamundi',ml='കരിം ചാമുണ്ഡി',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A fierce, dark form of the warrior goddess Chamundi.',
 alias=['Kari Chamundi','Karinchamundi','Karim Chamundi','Karinjamundi','Karimchamundi Theyyam'],tags=['chamundi','dark','black','warrior','palm leaf','fierce','kali'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Name means','The dark Chamundi'),('Family of','The Chamundi Theyyams'),('Role','Protective, fierce mother goddess')],
 who='Karimchamundi is one of the best-known Chamundi forms in North Malabar. “Kari” or “karim” means dark or black, so the name is usually understood as the dark Chamundi. Like the other forms, she is a fierce, protective mother goddess who is honoured for guarding a family or place.',
 story=['Chamundi is identified with Kali and Durga in the older stories, where she overcomes the demons Chanda and Munda. In local tradition each Chamundi form has its own story and shrine. We have not found one settled account of Karimchamundi that all sources agree on, and the story differs between families and shrines, so we do not repeat one here.'],
 look='Karimchamundi appears in a large costume of palm-leaf strips with a painted face and a decorated headdress. In photographs, the long palm-leaf skirt forms a wide, rounded shape.',
 trad='Chamundi shrines are often kept by families and ruling clans. The ritual follows older customs, including offerings that differ from those of many temples.',
 note='Fierce goddesses are approached with respect and some distance. Let your guide place you.',
 src=[('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam')]),
dict(slug='kurathi',name='Kurathi Amma',ml='കുറത്തി അമ്മ',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A protective mother goddess, loved by the women of the household.',
 alias=['Kurathi','Kurathiyamma','Kurathi Theyyam','Pulli Kurathi','Kunharu Kurathi','Kunnin Makal'],tags=['mother','parvati','protection','women','tharavadu','household','family','mountain'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Also known as','Kurathiyamma, Pulli Kurathi'),('Often linked with','Parvati, the “daughter of the mountain”'),('Role','Protector of the family and its home')],
 who='Kurathi Amma is a much-loved goddess of North Malabar, worshipped in family homes, village groves and temples. “Kurathi” is a word for a woman of the hill people, and she is often understood as a form of Parvati, the daughter of the mountain. Scholars note that goddesses such as Kurathi and Chamundi are especially honoured in the household shrines of ruling families.',
 story=['Tradition describes her as a guardian of the family and the home who guards against harm of many kinds. Women have a particular devotion to her, and they often prepare food to be offered to her.','In a story told about a related goddess, Unnanga Bhagavathi, a farmer’s sister dies after eating betel leaf from a plot that Kurathi had been asked to guard. When the farmer cries out, Kurathi draws coal from the funeral pyre with her sword, and a new goddess rises from it. This is a local telling and differs from place to place.'],
 look='Kurathi Amma wears a tall red headdress with round ornaments on either side, a bright orange-red painted face and a skirt of palm-leaf strips. Her bearing is dignified and watchful.',
 trad='She is reportedly the first Theyyam to appear at many shrines. Devotees ask for protection and for the wellbeing of the family and its fields.',
 note='Many women come forward to speak to Kurathi Amma. Give them space, and keep your voice low.',
 src=[('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam'),('Hindu Blog: Pulli Kurathi Amma Theyyam','https://www.hindu-blog.com/2023/05/pulli-kurathi-amma-theyyam-story.html')]),
dict(slug='kandanar-kelan',name='Kandanar Kelan',ml='കണ്ടനാർ കേളൻ',cat='heroes',
 tag='A devoted farmer who was lost in a forest fire and restored to life as a deity.',
 alias=['Kandanar Kelan Theyyam','Kandanaar Kelan','Kelan','Agni Pravesham'],tags=['farmer','forest','snake','fire','vayanattu kulavan','thiyya','resurrection','embers'],
 facts=[('Category','Hero deity'),('Real name','Kelan, a farmer'),('Restored by','The hunter god Vayanattu Kulavan'),('Performed by','Traditionally the Malaya community')],
 who='Kandanar Kelan is a Theyyam of North Malabar that remembers a farmer named Kelan. The name is explained as “the one found in the forest fire”. Tellings agree on the outline and differ in details, so please treat the following as a general summary of oral tradition.',
 story=['In one version Kelan is a foundling raised by a childless woman of the Thiyya community, and as a grown man he cleared the forest for her to farm. While clearing yet another piece of land, a fire broke out. He climbed a tree to escape, but snakes were among its branches. The tree burned and fell, and Kelan and the snakes died.','The hunter god Vayanattu Kulavan came upon the ashes, touched the form there with his bow, and Kelan was restored to life. From then on he was honoured as a deity. One account says he set the fire himself while clearing land after drinking, while another says it was an accident. The sources do not settle which.'],
 look='Descriptions mention a red and gold costume, heavy anklets whose sound marks the deity’s presence, a face painted in red and black, and a headdress placed on the performer to mark the arrival of the god. Two painted serpents on the chest recall the snakes in the story.',
 trad='The performance includes a passage called Agni Pravesham, in which the performer enters a fire of coconut leaves, and the origin song is sung before it. Afterwards the deity receives devotees and gives blessings. The deity is tied to the Thiyya community by the story, and the Theyyam is traditionally performed by the Malaya community.',
 note='Like other fire rituals, this is dangerous. Keep behind the boundary and follow your guide’s instructions.',
 src=[('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam'),('Kerala Folklore: Kandanar Kelan Theyyam','https://keralafolklore.com/kandanar-kelan-theyyam.html')]),

dict(slug='vayanattu-kulavan',name='Vayanattu Kulavan',ml='വയനാട്ടുകുലവൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='A blind hunter god, honoured as the chief deity of many Thiyya families.',
 alias=['Vayanat Kulavan','Vayanattu Kulavan Theyyam','Adhitheeyan','Thondachan','Kulavan'],tags=['hunter','shiva','toddy','blind','mirror','thiyya','bow','kandanar kelan','wayanad'],
 facts=[('Category','God (hunter deity)'),('Also known as','Adhitheeyan, Thondachan'),('Said to be','A son of Shiva'),('Worshipped by','Mainly the Thiyya community')],
 who='Vayanattu Kulavan is a major deity of North Malabar who appears as a hunter, and is the principal deity of many Thiyya families. Other communities also revere him. He is also called Adhitheeyan and Thondachan, which means ancestor.',
 story=['In the best-known telling, he is a son of Shiva, born when Shiva struck his thigh. He gathered toddy for his father, but Parvati had him sent away from heaven. On earth he drank toddy that was set aside for Shiva, and Shiva punished him with blindness. Shiva offered silver eyes and a lamp in return, but Kulavan cast them away, and where they fell, he was honoured as a god.','A second telling is about a man who strayed into a forbidden forest, became an attendant of Shiva, and was blinded after drinking the reserved toddy. In both stories the blind god is said to see people’s hearts and sorrows, and in the story of Kandanar Kelan, it is Kulavan’s bow that restores the farmer to life.'],
 look='Kulavan wears a heavy headdress made from natural materials, richly painted face art and a layered costume. The performer’s eyes are shown closed or hidden, reflecting the blind god. Before the deity appears, the performer looks into a mirror, which is the moment when the god is said to take form.',
 trad='Performers are drawn from hereditary families. The ritual can last many hours or a full night, and the deity is honoured as the family’s central protector.',
 note='Many families treat Kulavan as their own household deity. Take your lead from the family hosting the ritual.',
 src=[('Kerala Folklore: Vayanattu Kulavan Theyyam','https://keralafolklore.com/vayanattu-kulavan-theyyam.html'),KT]),
dict(slug='bhairavan',name='Bhairavan',ml='ഭൈരവൻ',cat='gods',
 tag='The fierce wandering form of Shiva, who begs with a skull.',
 alias=['Bhairava','Bhairavan Theyyam','Kala Bhairava','Kapala Bhairava','Kapaladhari'],tags=['shiva','ascetic','skull','brahma','paanan','malayan','night','mantra murti','fierce'],
 facts=[('Category','God (Shaiva tradition)'),('Form of','Shiva as the fierce ascetic'),('Performed by','The Paanan and Malayan communities'),('Performed','Usually at night')],
 who='Bhairavan is the fierce, wandering form of Shiva, who is shown as an ascetic. He is the chief deity of the Paanan community, and artists of the Malayan community also perform him. Folklore writers place him among the deities that need especially careful preparation.',
 story=['One story links him to Shiva as Kapaladhari, the skull-bearer. After Shiva cut off the fifth head of Brahma, he was cursed to carry the skull and beg. The paradox of a beggar who is also the ruler of the cosmos is central to the Theyyam. The Paanan tradition draws on the Tamil text Periya Puranam, though we have not found which episodes.'],
 look='The face is painted with natural colours in a fierce pattern, and the costume is rich and red, with a very tall headdress. The performance is usually at night and can end in the early hours.',
 trad='The preparation takes many hours and includes an invocation song, face painting, dressing and the placing of the headdress, which is understood as the moment when the deity arrives.',
 note='Bhairavan is a fierce form. Keep your voice low and give the performer space.',
 src=[('Kerala Folklore: Bhairavan Theyyam','https://keralafolklore.com/bhairavan-theyyam.html'),KT]),
dict(slug='puli-daivam',name='Puli Daivam',ml='പുലി ദൈവങ്ങൾ',cat='gods',
 tag='The tiger deities of North Malabar, born to Shiva and Parvati in tiger form.',
 alias=['Puli Daivangal','Pulidaivam','Tiger Theyyam','Puliyoor Kali','Pulikandan','Pulikarinkali','Marapuli','Kandapuli','Pulimaruthan','Puliyoor Kannan'],tags=['tiger','tigress','shiva','parvati','forest','thamburatti','vannan','puli'],
 facts=[('Category','God and goddess (a family of deities)'),('Includes','Puliyoor Kali, Pulikandan, Pulikarinkali and others'),('Story','Shiva and Parvati as a tiger and tigress'),('Puliyoor Kali performed by','The Vannan community')],
 who='Puli Daivam, the tiger deities, are a family of Theyyams of North Malabar. “Puli” means tiger. Kerala Tourism lists several of them: Puliyoor Kali, Pulikarinkali, Pulikandan, Marapuli, Kandapuli and Pulimaruthan.',
 story=['The stories say that Shiva and Parvati, passing through a forest, saw tigers and took the forms of a tiger, Pulikandan, and a tigress, Pulikarinkali. The tigress gave birth in a cave beneath a rock, and the cubs became tiger deities. The best known is a daughter, Puliyoor Kali. The sons are named variously, and the number of children differs between accounts.','Puliyoor Kali is often performed alone as a companion deity in a shrine. When she appears with her mother, the mother is called Valiya Thamburatti and the daughter Cheriya Thamburatti. In some places she is performed together with Puliyoor Kannan. These details come mainly from popular sources, so please treat them as one tradition among several.'],
 look='Puliyoor Kali wears a very tall, round headdress, an orange-red painted face and a richly layered red costume. She is described as fierce, with angry postures and graceful steps and spins, and at one point the sacred headdress is lowered to touch the ground in reverence.',
 trad='Puliyoor Kali is performed by the Vannan community. The tiger deities are understood as protectors, and are approached with respect.',
 note='Pulimaranja Thondachan, who is described in a separate page, is a different tiger deity with a different story.',
 src=[('Hindu Blog: Puliyoor Kali Theyyam','https://www.hindu-blog.com/2023/05/puliyoor-kali-theyyam-story-information.html'),KT]),
dict(slug='vishakandan',name='Vishakandan',ml='വിഷകണ്ഠൻ',cat='heroes',
 tag='A healer of snakebite, honoured after he was killed out of envy.',
 alias=['Vishakanda','Vishakandan Theyyam'],tags=['snake','snakebite','healer','poison','thiyya','kandan','physician','mantra'],
 facts=[('Category','Hero and healer'),('Real name','Kandan, of the Thiyya community'),('Linked with','Healing from poison and snakebite'),('Story','Jealousy, a death and a restless spirit')],
 who='Vishakandan is a rare Theyyam that remembers Kandan, a Thiyya man who was known for curing snakebites. “Visha” means poison. We have found only one source for the story, so please treat it as local tradition.',
 story=['The story says that a famous physician declared a woman bitten by a snake to be dead, and her relatives took her away for cremation. Kandan saw the body and judged that she was alive. He had it placed in water, she revived, and he chanted a mantra from a coconut tree as part of the cure.','As Kandan’s fame grew, the physician grew jealous, and had him killed after inviting him to dinner. Misfortunes followed in the household, and astrologers advised that Kandan’s restless spirit should be honoured as a deity with a Theyyam every year.'],
 look='The performer wears a red costume with a tall, rounded headdress and a painted face. We did not find a detailed description in our sources.',
 trad='The story is about healing, injustice and the settling of an unquiet spirit. Devotees may pray to him for protection from poison and illness.',
 note='Stories like this one are told with feeling. Listen quietly.',
 src=[('Hindu Blog: Vishakandan Theyyam','https://www.hindu-blog.com/2023/06/vishakandan-theyyam-story-information.html'),KT]),
dict(slug='puthiya-bhagavathi',name='Puthiya Bhagavathi',ml='പുതിയ ഭഗവതി',cat='goddesses',
 tag='A goddess said to have risen from a sacrificial fire, and the main deity of many Kannur shrines.',
 alias=['Puthya Bhagavathi','Puthiya Bhagavathy','Puthiya Bhagavathi Theyyam','Cheerumba Bhagavathi','Puthyayotra'],tags=['bhadrakali','fire','smallpox','shiva','mother','cheerumba','kannur'],
 facts=[('Category','Goddess'),('Often identified with','Bhadrakali'),('Younger sister','Cheerumba Bhagavathi'),('Accompanied by','Padakulangara Veeran and Veerakali (in some tellings)')],
 who='Puthiya Bhagavathi is a goddess who is the main deity of many shrines in the Kannur region. She is generally identified with Bhadrakali, and Cheerumba Bhagavathi is regarded as her younger sister.',
 story=['According to Kerala Tourism’s account, she emerged from a sacrificial fire that was lit to cure Shiva of smallpox. In some tellings she is accompanied by the warrior figures Padakulangara Veeran and Veerakali.'],
 look='She wears a very large, fan-shaped headdress made of tender coconut leaf, edged in yellow-green, with a bright red centre, over a skirt of palm-leaf strips and a painted red face.',
 trad='She appears at many shrines in the region, and her Theyyam is also associated with ritual lamps.',
 note='The headdress is wide and the performer turns often. Keep clear.',
 src=[('Kerala Tourism: Various types of Theyyam','https://keralatourism.org/bekal/theyyam-various-types.php'),('Theyyam','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theyyam')]),
]
